<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">British Ceylon</h1><p class="st-meta">1796 CE – 1948 CE · capital Colombo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British Crown Colony of Ceylon, which unified the island under one administration and built a plantation economy of coffee, tea and rubber.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>British forces from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/madras-presidency">Madras Presidency</a> took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> coastal provinces of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-ceylon">Dutch Ceylon</a> in 1795 to 1796, and they became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown Colony</a> in 1802. In 1815 the British, exploiting discontent among Kandyan chiefs, took over the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kandy">Kingdom of Kandy</a>, bringing the whole island under one rule for the first time.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colebrooke-cameron-commission">Colebrooke-Cameron</a> reforms of 1833 centralised administration. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">Coffee</a> plantations spread in the hills until blight in the 1870s, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and coconut dominated, worked by Tamil labourers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. English education created a new elite, and a Buddhist revival led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anagarika-dharmapala">Anagarika Dharmapala</a> grew. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donoughmore-constitution">Donoughmore Constitution</a> of 1931 introduced universal suffrage.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> contributed to the Allied effort in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soulbury-constitution">Soulbury Constitution</a> prepared self-government. It gained independence peacefully in 1948 as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-ceylon">Dominion of Ceylon</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/d-s-senanayake">D. S. Senanayake</a> as first prime minister.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-ceylon.jpg" alt="British Ceylon" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin (original) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ceylon_(1875%E2%80%931948).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1796 CE – 1948 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Colombo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick North, Robert Brownrigg, Henry Ward</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism, Hinduism, Islam, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sinhala, Tamil, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Crown Colony of Ceylon, British Sri Lanka</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1872&amp;focus=british-ceylon" aria-label="British Ceylon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6891 1919 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where British Ceylon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7211 2175L7211 2175M7211 2175L4997 651M7211 2175L7231 2161M7211 2175L7211 2175M7211 2175L7039 2277"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7211 2175h0M4997 651h0M7231 2161h0M7211 2175h0M7039 2277h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7211 2175h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7211 2175h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Colombo. <a href="/atlas?year=1872&amp;focus=british-ceylon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
